{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RegTraceMatrix","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RegTraceMatrix","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RegTraceGramNondegenerate","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ToLin"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_stdBasis_det_ne_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_stdBasis_det_ne_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L172-L187","doc":"**The block Gram determinant vanishes exactly in the bad characteristic.**\n\n`d^(d²) = 0` in `K` iff `d = 0` in `K`, so this is the sharp form of the obstruction that\n`docs/audits/2026-08-30-tw-gram-determinant-measured.md` states — and the reason the\ndeterminant route can never certify semisimplicity when `char K ∣ d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_stdBasis_det","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_stdBasis_det","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L157-L170","doc":"**The block Gram determinant is `± d^(d²)`, sorry-free.**\n\nThis is where the integer constant of the Gram determinant comes from.\n`computations/hecke/tw_gram_determinant_probe.py` measures that constant as\n`∏_λ d_λ^(d_λ²)` at `n = 1, 2, 3` and identifies it empirically; the per-block factor\n`d^(d²)` is derived here, from `tr(E_ij · E_kl) = δ_{jk} δ_{il}` and nothing else.\n\nThe sign is the signature of the transpose involution on `ι × ι`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_stdBasis_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_stdBasis_eq","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L144-L155","doc":"**The Gram matrix of a block, in the matrix-unit basis, is `d •` the identity with its\nrows permuted by the transpose involution.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_single","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L131-L142","doc":"**The matrix-unit Gram entries**: `tr(E_ij · E_kl) = δ_{jk} δ_{il}`, so the regular trace\nform pairs `E_ij` with `E_ji` and nothing else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.card_mul_trace_one_eq_finrank_matrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.card_mul_trace_one_eq_finrank_matrix","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L117-L127","doc":"**Consistency check between the two routes, kept as a regression.**\n\n`regTraceForm_one_one` (proved from `L_1 = id` and `LinearMap.trace_id`) says\n`B(1,1) = finrank K A`.  `regTraceForm_matrix` (proved from the column block decomposition\nand `trace_piDiag`) says `B(1,1) = d · tr 1 = d · d`.  The two arguments share nothing, so\ntheir agreement — `finrank K (Matrix ι ι K) = d²` — is a real cross-check on both.  A dropped\nfactor or a wrong index set in either would show up here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_matrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_matrix","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L108-L115","doc":"**The regular trace form of a matrix algebra is `d ·` the matrix trace.**\n\nThe sharp form of what `regTraceForm_one_one` gives only on the diagonal.  Every statement\nthis corpus makes about the form degenerating \"exactly when `char K ∣ d`\" is a corollary of\nthis together with nondegeneracy of `tr`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.trace_lmul_matrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.trace_lmul_matrix","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L94-L106","doc":"**The trace of left multiplication on a matrix algebra**: `card ι` copies of the ordinary\nmatrix trace, one per column. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.colEquiv_lmul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.colEquiv_lmul","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L82-L92","doc":"**Left multiplication is block-diagonal over the columns.**\n\nColumn `j` of `M * X` is `M *ᵥ (column j of X)`, with the *same* `M` in every block — so\n`lmul M` is `card ι` copies of `M.mulVecLin`, conjugated by `colEquiv`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.colEquiv_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.colEquiv_apply","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L79-L80","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.colEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.colEquiv","kind":"def","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceMatrix.lean#L67-L76","doc":"**Reading a square matrix by its columns**: `colEquiv K ι X j` is column `j` of `X`.\n\nThis is the identification under which left multiplication becomes block-diagonal. ","signatureChunk":0}]}